        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Jutland</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">31 May to 1 June 1916, in extensive manoeuvring and three main engagements</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> Peninsula, near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s Grand Fleet under Admiral Sir John Jellicoe, with Vice-Admiral Sir David Beatty commanding its battlecruisers, against the Imperial German Navy's High Seas Fleet under Vice-Admiral Reinhard Scheer, with Vice-Admiral Franz Hipper commanding its scouting force; the two fleets totalled 250 ships</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">Fourteen British and eleven German ships sank, with 9,823 dead in all. The Germans broke through the British rear in the night and returned to port; both sides claimed victory, and there is no consensus on which nation won, or whether either did.</span></div>
