    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The battle was the largest naval battle and the only full-scale clash of battleships of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the last major naval battle in any war fought primarily by battleships.</li><li>The British lost 14 ships and the Germans 11, and the British lost more sailors, yet the High Seas Fleet never again sought a fleet action, and the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> continued.</li><li>Both sides claimed victory, and there is no consensus over which nation was victorious, or whether there was a victor at all.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A copy of the main German codebook, taken from the light cruiser SMS Magdeburg after she ran aground in Russian waters in 1914, meant the British Admiralty could often read German naval signals, and it knew a major German operation was likely before Scheer sailed.</p></aside>
    </section>

