      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">January 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Scheer replaces the ill Admiral von Pohl as commander of the German fleet</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1916</span><span class="rf-text">The German Admiralty halts indiscriminate submarine attacks on merchant shipping after neutral protests</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Jellicoe sails from Scapa Flow on 30 May after the Admiralty intercepts Scheer's signal</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Beatty and Hipper meet on 31 May; Indefatigable and Queen Mary are lost in the battlecruiser action</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Jellicoe deploys at 18:15, and the main fleets engage twice before dark</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">June 1916</span><span class="rf-text">Scheer breaks through the British rear in the night and the German fleet reaches port</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1916</span><span class="rf-text">A German advance into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> is nearly intercepted by the Grand Fleet</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">April 1917</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declares war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after unrestricted submarine warfare resumes</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-jutland" data-v="45033f82a5" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The fleets sail towards each other unseen</b> Beatty's ships ran east while Hipper's battlecruisers ran north, with Scheer's battle line following Hipper. Jellicoe's fleet was still out of sight to the north-west, and the Germans did not know it was at sea.</li><li><b>The Run to the South</b> Hipper turned south-east to lead Beatty towards Scheer and opened fire. The British battlecruisers, with the 5th Battle Squadron behind them, followed on the south-western side. Hood was sent ahead from Jellicoe's fleet at about 16:05.</li><li><b>Beatty turns north and the Germans follow</b> Beatty sighted the German battle line and turned his battlecruisers about to lead the Germans towards Jellicoe. The 5th Battle Squadron passed them going the other way and then turned north, drawing the fire of Hipper and of Scheer's leading battleships.</li><li><b>Jellicoe deploys to the east</b> Jellicoe formed his six columns into a single battle line, deploying on the eastern column. Beatty's battlecruisers crossed the front of his line to join Hood, and Hood and Beatty engaged Hipper while Scheer's fleet came on from the south.</li><li><b>Scheer's T is crossed and he turns away</b> The leading German battleships came out of the mist facing the whole British line, which crossed their T. Scheer ordered a turn together through 180°, and the German fleet disengaged under smoke and mist.</li><li><b>Scheer turns back and turns away again</b> Scheer doubled back east, and by 19:15 Jellicoe had crossed his T again. Scheer turned west a second time and sent his battlecruisers and torpedo boats at the British to cover the turn.</li><li><b>Scheer slips round the British rear in the night</b> Jellicoe headed south with a screen of cruisers and destroyers behind him to guard Scheer's expected escape route. Scheer instead crossed the British wake and made for Horns Reef, passing through the screen.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>With 16 dreadnought-type battleships against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>'s 28, the German High Seas Fleet stood little chance in a head-to-head clash. The Germans adopted a divide-and-conquer strategy, raiding into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> to lure out small British squadrons that superior forces or submarines could destroy. In January 1916 Admiral von Pohl fell ill and was replaced by Scheer, who believed the fleet had been used too defensively. The aim belonged to a larger strategy of breaking the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The Royal Navy sought to engage and destroy the High Seas Fleet, or at least to keep it contained and away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and its shipping lanes.</p><p>On 25 April 1916 the German Imperial Admiralty halted indiscriminate submarine attacks on merchant shipping after protests from neutral countries, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and Scheer deployed the submarines against military vessels instead. Ten were stationed off British naval bases to catch the ships that a raid on Sunderland was meant to draw out. Delays and north-easterly winds that ruled out the Zeppelins led the Germans to choose a battlecruiser sortie into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a>, and the signal "31 May G.G.2490" was sent.</p><p>The British had obtained the main German codebook from the light cruiser Magdeburg, so German radio traffic could often be read. By 17:00 on 30 May the Admiralty had the signal "31 May G.G.2490". Not knowing the German objective, Jellicoe sailed from Scapa Flow at 22:30 that day to take a position off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Jutland&#8221; cites where it covers German planning, British response:</p><ul><li>Mark Stille, British Dreadnought vs. German Dreadnought: Jutland 1916. (Osprey Publishing, 2010)</li><li>Andrew Gordon, The Rules of the Game: Jutland and British Naval Command (John Murray, 1996)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jellicoe's Grand Fleet was split in two. The Battle Fleet, with which he sailed, comprised 24 battleships, three battlecruisers, eight armoured cruisers, eight light cruisers, four scout cruisers, 51 destroyers and a destroyer-minelayer. Beatty's Battlecruiser Fleet comprised six battlecruisers, four fast Queen Elizabeth-class battleships, 14 light cruisers and 27 destroyers, with the seaplane tender Engadine. The British ships' total weight of broadside was 332,360 lb (150,760 kg).</p><p>Scheer's main fleet had 16 battleships, six pre-dreadnoughts, six light cruisers and 31 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats. Hipper's scouting force had five battlecruisers, five light cruisers and 30 torpedo boats. The German total weight of broadside was 134,216 lb (60,879 kg). The six slow pre-dreadnoughts limited the German fleet to 18 knots, against a British maximum of 21. British battlecruisers traded armour for speed and heavier guns, while German designs favoured armour and survivability.</p><p>Fleets of the time deployed from columns into a single battle line, ideally crossing the enemy's path, "crossing the T", so that most guns could fire while the enemy could use only its forward guns. Jellicoe meant to meet Beatty about 90 miles (140 km) west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a>. Hipper was to lure Beatty's squadrons into the path of the main German fleet.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At 14:28 on 31 May the first shots were fired, between British and German light forces. Beatty's signals to Evan-Thomas's four battleships were not received, leaving them 10 miles (16 km) behind instead of five. Hipper sighted Beatty at 15:22 and turned south-east at 15:45 to lead him toward Scheer. At 15:48 Hipper opened fire, beginning the Run to the South. At 16:02 Indefatigable blew up, leaving two survivors from 1,019. At 16:25 Queen Mary blew up, with all but nine of 1,275 lost. Goodenough's Southampton sighted the High Seas Fleet at about 16:30, and at 16:40 Beatty turned north toward Jellicoe.</p><p>Jellicoe, uncertain of the Germans' position, ordered deployment to the east at 18:15. Defence blew up with all hands, 903 officers and men. At 18:30 Invincible was sunk in 90 seconds, all but six of 1,032 killed, Hood among them. At 18:30 the main fleets engaged, with Jellicoe crossing Scheer's T, and Scheer turned away at 18:33. He doubled back at 18:55, and at 19:17 turned away a second time, covered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats and a charge by Hipper's four remaining battlecruisers. Jellicoe turned away from the torpedoes.</p><p>At 21:00 Jellicoe headed south, hoping to renew the fight at dawn, but Scheer crossed his wake toward Horns Reef. The British rear met the Germans seven times in the night, and few reports reached Jellicoe. Pommern blew up at 03:10 with all hands, 839 officers and men. Jellicoe learned Scheer's position only at 04:15, too late to resume.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Jutland&#8221; cites where it covers Order of battle, Battlecruiser action, Contact, Run to the south:</p><ul><li>Arthur J. Marder, Jutland and after, May 1916 – December 1916 (Oxford University Press, 1966)</li><li>John Brooks, Dreadnought Gunnery at the Battle of Jutland: The Question of Fire Control (Routledge, Frank Cass series, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/winston-churchill.jpg" alt="Winston Churchill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Winston_Churchill_-_19086236948_(restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fourteen British and eleven German ships sank, with a total of 9,823 casualties. One set of figures gives British losses of 6,784 and German of 3,039; another gives 6,094 British and 2,551 German seamen lost. The British lost three battlecruisers, three armoured cruisers, a flotilla leader and seven destroyers, about 113,300 tons by one count or 115,000 long tons by another. The Germans lost the battlecruiser Lützow, the pre-dreadnought Pommern, four light cruisers and five destroyers, about 62,300 tons or 62,000 long tons.</p><p>On 2 June the German authorities claimed a victory, withholding the loss of Lützow, Elbing and Rostock. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> announced a new chapter in world history. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the first news came from German wireless broadcasts, and the Admiralty's bare statement of 2 June led newspapers to report a German victory. The public was shocked. Arthur Balfour asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> to write a second, more positive report. On 7 June the German admission of the loss of Lützow and Rostock began to change the picture towards a qualified British victory.</p><p>Most German losses were made good within a month, and one month after the battle the Grand Fleet was stronger than before it sailed. The Germans made only three more sorties, in August and October 1916 and April 1918, all quickly aborted, and Jellicoe prohibited the Grand Fleet from steaming south of Horns Reef.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Jutland&#8221; cites where it covers Reporting, British self-critique, Aviation:</p><ul><li>Arthur J. Marder, Jutland and after, May 1916 – December 1916 (Oxford University Press, 1966)</li><li>Lawrence Burr, British Battlecruisers 1914–18 (Osprey Publishing, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Many details come from the participants. Scheer wrote memoirs, Commander von Hase, a gunnery officer of Derfflinger, described the loss of Queen Mary, and Jellicoe's official despatch and the Admiralty's examination record the British side. There is no official record of the order popularly said to have followed Beatty's remark about his ships, to turn two points to port.</p><p>The wrecks add physical evidence. From 2000 to 2016 expeditions involving Innes McCartney located all the wrecks sunk in the battle, and over 60 per cent had suffered from metal theft. A 2003 survey of Invincible, Queen Mary, Defence and Lützow showed that P and Q turrets of Invincible were blasted out before she broke in half, and found cordite containers stacked in the working chamber of Queen Mary's X turret. A major part of the blame for the British explosions may thus be laid on lax handling of cordite.</p><p>The result is still argued. Some historians support the German claim of victory, because the Germans inflicted greater losses and never meant to hold the site. Others see a strategic British victory, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept command of the area and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>. A third view holds that the battle showed the irrelevance of battleship fleets after the submarine, mine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a>. There is no clear consensus.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Jutland&#8221; cites where it covers Controversy:</p><ul><li>John Brooks, The Battle of Jutland (Cambridge University Press, 2016)</li><li>Keith Yates, Flawed victory : Jutland, 1916 (Naval Institute Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Jellicoe was criticised at the time for caution and for letting Scheer escape, as the British had hoped for another Trafalgar. He became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sea-lord">First Sea Lord</a>, and Beatty replaced him as commander of the Grand Fleet. The controversy lasted about a decade after the war. Jellicoe's supporters, among them the historian Cyril Falls, pointed to the folly of risking defeat when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> already held command of the sea, while others condemned Beatty's handling of the first encounter.</p><p>The battle led to changes. British armour-piercing shells often failed to penetrate German armour, and new shells came in April 1918 and were never used in action. After the loss of three battlecruisers the First Lord told Cabinet members on 5 June 1916 that unsafe cordite management was to blame, and standing orders on independent action and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> use were revised.</p><p>Scheer concluded that further battles of the same <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attrition">attrition</a> would exhaust his fleet. At the end of 1916 the German Navy turned to unrestricted submarine warfare, which, with the Zimmermann Telegram, led by April 1917 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> declaring war. The battle was celebrated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as a victory, in Weimar Germany by the right wing, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmshaven">Wilhelmshaven</a> with torch-lit parades until the end of the 1960s. The light cruiser HMS Caroline survives as a museum ship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Jutland&#8221; cites where it covers Assessments, Remembrance:</p><ul><li>Jonathan Sutherland, The Battle of Jutland (Pen &amp; Sword Maritime, 2007)</li><li>Arthur J. Marder, Jutland and after, May 1916 – December 1916 (Oxford University Press, 1966)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/its-a-long-way-to-tipperary">It's a Long Way to Tipperary<span>1912</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> on 31 May and 1 June 1916 the British Grand Fleet and the German High Seas Fleet fought the largest naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> lost more ships and men, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s fleet returned to port and did not seek another fleet action; both sides claimed victory.</em></p>

